  "step_3": "The decision reason requires identifying which lottery is more right-skewed (i.e., assigns higher probability to unfavorable outcomes). Lottery A assigns 80% to the favorable outcome (4000€), so it is not right-skewed. The individual, however, sees this 80% chance of a high gain as a positive feature, interpreting it as 'lucrative', which contradicts the mindset of someone preferring right-skewed lotteries (who would favor higher chance of bad outcomes). The individual’s focus is on the attractiveness of a high payoff with high probability, not on distributional asymmetry or a preference for downside risk. There is no alignment between the information used by the individual (magnitude and likelihood of gains) and the information required for the skewness reason (likelihood of unfavorable vs. favorable outcomes). Furthermore, the justification based on 'lucrativeness' contradicts the logic of preferring right-skewed lotteries. Therefore, it is certain that the individual did not use the skewness-based reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
